WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.820
What does an operating system

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.920
actually do? It manages the

3
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.720
hardware and gives programs useful

4
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.940
services, so every application does

5
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.100
not have to control the

6
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.960
machine itself. Calling it the

7
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.060
thing that opens your apps

8
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.320
is a little vague, darling.

9
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.320
Start with the layers: hardware

10
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.620
supplies the processor, memory, and

11
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.780
input-output devices. The operating system

12
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.940
controls and coordinates those resources,

13
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.280
while applications use its services

14
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.140
to solve your tasks. The

15
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.540
kernel is the core part

16
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:30.720
of that operating system.

17
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.880
Think of the operating system

18
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.440
as both a resource allocator

19
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.340
and a control program. It

20
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.240
decides who gets resources, and

21
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.780
controls how programs use the

22
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.240
machine. User convenience and efficient

23
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.880
hardware use are two goals,

24
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.100
and they can pull in

25
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:45.900
different directions.

26
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.900
Let us make that concrete

27
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.140
with two jobs, A and

28
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.040
B, both available at time

29
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.220
zero. Job A needs two

30
00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.280
units on the processor, then

31
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.400
four units of input-output, then

32
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.820
two more processor units. B

33
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.840
needs four processor units, and

34
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.180
our example ignores switching overhead.

35
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.200
In a simple one-job-at-a-time system,

36
00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.760
A runs on the processor

37
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.840
from zero to two. Then

38
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.520
A waits for input-output from

39
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.880
two to six, so the

40
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.340
processor sits idle. A finally

41
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.880
runs again from six to

42
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:19.180
eight.

43
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.580
Only then does B use

44
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:22.960
the processor, from eight to

45
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.320
twelve. That gives eight units

46
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.220
of useful processor work across

47
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.380
twelve elapsed units. Processor utilization

48
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.220
is eight divided by twelve,

49
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.160
or about sixty-seven percent.

50
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.240
Multiprogramming keeps multiple jobs in

51
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.820
main memory so the processor

52
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.140
has another ready job to

53
00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.300
choose. A can remain in

54
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.620
memory while it waits for

55
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.480
input-output. Waiting does not mean

56
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.820
A is ready to execute

57
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.900
processor instructions.

58
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.720
Now replay the same workload

59
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.700
on one processor. A runs

60
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.500
from zero to two, then

61
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.180
starts its four-unit input-output operation.

62
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.660
The operating system switches the

63
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.520
processor to B, which runs

64
00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:03.880
from two to six.

65
00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.240
At time six, A's input-output

66
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.000
completes and B has finished.

67
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.080
A becomes ready, is selected,

68
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.640
and runs its last two

69
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.980
units from six to eight.

70
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.500
Watch the separate lanes: A's

71
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.880
device operation overlaps B's processor

72
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.260
work.

73
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.680
The useful processor work is

74
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.580
still eight units, but now

75
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.240
the elapsed time is eight

76
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.700
units too. Eight divided by

77
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.600
eight gives one hundred percent

78
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.840
utilization in this ideal example.

79
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.300
We improved the schedule; we

80
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.940
did not add another processor.

81
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.340
That is the opportunity multiprogramming

82
00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.800
creates, not a promise that

83
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.760
the processor never idles. If

84
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.160
every job is waiting for

85
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.380
a device, there may be

86
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.240
nothing ready to run. Switching

87
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.000
between jobs also takes time,

88
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.340
unlike the zero-overhead model we

89
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:50.960
just used.

90
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.240
Time sharing emphasizes responsiveness by

91
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.920
giving ready jobs frequent turns

92
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.680
on the processor. Here are

93
00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.120
two processor-only jobs alternating in

94
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.520
short time slices on one

95
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.800
core. They make concurrent progress,

96
00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.760
but only one executes on

97
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.960
that core at a time.

98
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.580
Multiprocessing adds hardware execution capacity:

99
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.300
multiple processors or processor cores.

100
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.980
Put two independent four-unit processor

101
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.580
jobs on two cores, and

102
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.860
both can run from zero

103
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.940
to four at the same

104
00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.220
time. On one core, those

105
00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.660
same eight units of processor

106
00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.000
work take eight units of

107
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:25.700
elapsed time.

108
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.280
These ideas can work together.

109
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.700
A multicore operating system can

110
00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.080
keep many jobs in memory

111
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.840
and schedule them across its

112
00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.760
cores. More jobs is a

113
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.760
statement about the workload; more

114
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.160
cores is a statement about

115
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:39.760
the hardware.

116
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.320
Things to take note of.

117
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.060
Multiprogramming helps use a processor

118
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.040
while another job waits; multiprocessing

119
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.300
allows work to execute on

120
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.840
multiple cores at once. Time

121
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.840
sharing emphasizes responsive turns, and

122
00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.720
concurrency alone does not prove

123
00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.980
physical parallelism.

124
00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:59.420
Quick check: A waits for

125
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:00.640
a device while B runs

126
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.340
on the only processor core.

127
00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.240
Is that multiprocessing? No: it

128
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.560
is the overlap that multiprogramming

129
00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.900
enables, with one job using

130
00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.720
the device and another using

131
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:11.680
the processor.

132
00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.640
For your examination, name the

133
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.200
resources, show the execution order,

134
00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.440
and explain the motivation. Distinguish

135
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.240
processor work from total time

136
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.120
before calculating utilization. There, a

137
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.900
proper explanation, instead of three

138
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.920
impressive words thrown into one

139
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:30.340
sentence.
